  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "finnish-folk-music-representative-fusions-and-scenes",
      "title": "Finnish Folk Music — Representative Fusions and Scenes",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Nordic Folk | **Region:** nordic-finland | **Tier:** 7\n**Prerequisites:** regions-nordic-finland-06-history\n**Tags:** nordic, finland, nordic, representative, fusions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Kaustinen Festival\n\nFinnish Folk Music belongs to the **Nordic Folk** curriculum. Maria Kalaniemi\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **Kaustinen Festival** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-norway",
      "title": "Compared with Norway",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Norwegian Folk Music** (`regions-nordic-norway-07-representative-depth`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **Kaustinen Festival**.\n\n| Topic | Norway anchor | Finnish Folk Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | concerts, festivals… | Kaustinen Festival… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-nordic-norway-07-representative-depth`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-07-representative-depth`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Kaustinen Festival\n\nStudy **finnish folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Finland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Finland) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Nordic Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **kantele, fiddle**. Ensemble layer: **accordion, bass**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **runo song, pelimanni tunes**. Dance context: **folk dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **Karelia, archives**. Contemporary scenes: **Kaustinen Festival**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Finnish pelimanni repertoire**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Kaustinen Festival\n- Lead instruments: kantele, fiddle\n- Ensemble: accordion, bass\n- Forms: runo song, pelimanni tunes\n- Dance: folk dance\n- History: Karelia, archives\n- Scenes: Kaustinen Festival\n- Repertoire: Finnish pelimanni repertoire\n- Compared with Norway: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Finnish Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: Kaustinen Festival.\n- Compared with Norway: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: runo song, pelimanni tunes.\n- Dance: folk dance.\n- Transmission: Karelia, archives."
        }
      ]
    },
